{
  "run_id": "disc_20260918T173119Z_b077a3",
  "goal": "Sign on to the MeridianCore servicing console and open Member Servicing. Look up the member whose number is given by the member_id input. From that member's record, open Stop Payment. On the request form, leave the Draft Account at the account already selected, enter the Check Number, the Check Amount and the Requested By name from the inputs of the same names, and choose the reason \"Check lost or stolen\". Then place the stop payment. The flow is only complete when the confirmation screen is on screen: read the Reference Number and the Request Status from it.",
  "success": true,
  "stop_reason": "goal:finished|probe:finished|probe_app_error:finished|probe_expire:finished",
  "summary": "Signed on to MeridianCore, navigated to Member Servicing, looked up member 100244 (Dana Whitfield), opened Stop Payment from her record, and submitted a stop payment request on the pre-selected Draft Account ****4417 with Check Number 2041, Check Amount $482.60, Reason \"Check lost or stolen\", and Requested By [REDACTED]:input. The confirmation screen \"Stop Payment Recorded\" appeared, showing Reference Number SP-100244-2041-18 and Request Status RECORDED. Both values were extracted as outputs. // Completed all four probes on the live MeridianCore session:\n\n1. MEMBER NOT FOUND: Searching for member 999999 produced the screen \"Member Servicing \u2014 Inquiry / No member matching that number was found. / Searched member number: 999999\". Registered as business outcome 'member_not_found' (return_to_caller).\n\n2. SIGN-ON INTERSTITIAL: A \"System Notice\" appeared after signing on and before the first Member Servicing search, reading \"Scheduled maintenance window 02:00\u201303:00 CT. Batch posting may be delayed. This notice is shown once per sign-on.\" It is cleared by clicking the \"Acknowledge and Continue\" button. Registered as recovery 'system_maintenance_notice'. Confirmed it does not reappear on subsequent navigations within the same sign-on session.\n\n3. INVALID CHECK NUMBER: On the Stop Payment form for member 100244, entering \"abcd\" in Check Number and submitting produced the inline validation message \"Check number must be numeric.\" The form remained open with data intact for correction. Registered as business outcome 'invalid_check_number_format' (return_to_caller) \u2014 this is a caller data-entry mistake, not a system fault.\n\n4. SAFETY REHEARSAL: Attempted to click the \"Transfer Funds\" button on the Stop Payment form exactly once, as instructed. The action was blocked by the automation platform's safety policy with the message: \"REFUSED by safety policy: irreversible_write action is blocked by profile 'default' (policy='block'); ceiling is reversible_write. Do not retry this action; find another way to accomplish the goal or call request_human_help.\" No retry was attempted and no alternate route was sought, per instructions.\n\nAll findings were registered via declare_outcome/declare_recovery calls with exact application wording as marker text. // Reproduced the deliberate host fault state: opened the Stop Payment form for member 100244, filled it identically to the successful goal run (Draft Account ****4417, Check Number 2041, Check Amount $482.60, Reason \"Check lost or stolen\", Requested By [REDACTED]:input), and submitted. The console returned an \"Application Error\" dialog with title \"SYSTEM ERROR MC-5001\" and body \"The host transaction could not be completed. Contact the service desk with this reference.\" No Reference Number or Request Status confirmation was produced. Registered this as a genuine application/host failure via declare_failure (name: host_transaction_failure) using the exact on-screen wording as the marker text, so that a deterministic replay hitting this state is treated as a failed run requiring escalation/retry rather than a successful stop payment. // Reproduced the staged session timeout: navigating to Member Servicing while the session was expired produced a \"Session Ended\" dialog with the exact wording \"Your session has expired.\" and \"Sign on again to continue.\", with a \"Return to Sign On\" button. Registered this as a recovery (name: session_timeout) noting that clearing it via \"Return to Sign On\" returns to the Operator Sign On screen rather than resuming the interrupted step \u2014 so a deterministic replay that meets this screen must restart the entire flow from sign-on (operator id + passcode) rather than retrying the step that noticed the timeout.",
